# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: numeric-edges-vendor-divergences -- integer overflow and atof hex-float, as AutoCAD / BricsCAD do
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: windows-runner-sunset-probe-campaign
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-29

Measured by the sunset campaign's B1 probe (probes/sources/probe-numeric-edges.lsp)
on real hardware 2026-10-24: AutoCAD 2022 + BricsCAD V26, committed under
probe-results/{autocad/ms-windows/20261024T201442Z,bricscad/macos/20261024T201604Z}/.
Two numeric divergences where clautolisp matches NEITHER vendor; one is a plain
bug (both vendors agree), one is a genuine AutoCAD-vs-BricsCAD split.

* 1. Integer overflow -- a real 3-way divergence  (dialect-resolved)

At/above 2^31, clautolisp SIGNALS "outside the AutoLISP 32-bit integer range",
AutoCAD keeps a 32-bit int and WRAPS (two's complement), and BricsCAD WIDENS to
a >=64-bit integer. Measured:

| expression            | AutoCAD           | BricsCAD        | clautolisp   |
|-----------------------+-------------------+-----------------+--------------|
| (+ 2147483640 100)    | -2147483556 (INT) | 2147483740 INT  | error        |
| (* 2000000000 2)      | -294967296  INT   | 4000000000  INT | error        |
| (1+ 2147483647)       | -2147483648 INT   | 2147483648  INT | error        |
| (* 46341 46341)       | -2147479015 INT   | 2147488281  INT | error        |
| (* 100000 100000)     | 1410065408  INT   | 10000000000 INT | error        |
| (- -2147483648 1)     | -2.14748e+09 REAL | -2147483649 INT | error        |

Note AutoCAD's asymmetry: overflow WRAPS, but the one underflow case
(=(- -2147483648 1)=) PROMOTES TO REAL. BricsCAD widens throughout.

Resolution (dialect-resolved, the project pattern): the arithmetic layer must,
when an integer result leaves the signed-32 range,
  - autocad / strict  -> wrap mod 2^32 to signed-32 (and, for the INT_MIN-1
    underflow, promote to a double), matching AutoCAD 2022;
  - bricscad / lax     -> widen (keep the exact integer), matching BricsCAD V26;
  - clautolisp         -> pjb's call: today it SIGNALS (safe, catches real
    overflow bugs), which is neither vendor. Keep-signal, or adopt one vendor?
This is NOT a small change -- it touches every +/-/*/1+/1- path -- so it is
scoped as its own work item, not folded into the quick fixes below.

* 2. atof C99 hex-float  (plain bug, both vendors agree)

=(atof "0x1p4")= is 16.0 on BOTH AutoCAD and BricsCAD (strtod accepts C99
hexadecimal-floating syntax); clautolisp returns 0.0. Decimal-comma is rejected
on all three (=(atof "3,5")= -> 3.0), and every other atof/atoi edge probed
agrees across the three (017->17, 0xbabe->0, 17x->17, " 17"->17, .5, 1., 1e3,
1.5e-3). So the ONLY atof divergence is hex-float, and clautolisp is simply
wrong against both vendors.

Fix: atof must parse like C strtod, which accepts =0x<hex>p<exp>=. Today
builtin-atof delegates to parse-autolisp-real (the Lisp real reader), which does
not do hex-float -- and the reader should NOT start accepting hex-float literals,
so atof needs its own strtod-compatible front (hex-float, then fall back to the
decimal path). All dialects (both vendors agree). Deferred here only because it
wants a small dedicated parser, not a one-liner.

* Status

TODO. Divergence 2 (atof hex-float) and Divergence 1 (integer overflow) are both
specified above with measured tables. Neither is in the "quick adopt" set of the
B2 vle fixes; both want their own focused change (atof: a strtod-style parser;
overflow: dialect-resolved arithmetic). Fold the measured values into the spec's
atof / number-tower "Tested Behaviour" when implemented.
